#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124216 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef65513d-1e9e-571f-8b93-4e2d63aed68e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subspaces of Function Vector Spaces"}] :question/id #uuid "69da2e7c-7ede-5966-8ff9-13bca51c0420" :question/math-academy-id "e-4813" :question/problem "Consider the vector space $\\mathcal{C}[-1,3]$ of all continuous functions $f:[-1,3] \\to \\mathbb{R}$ and its subset $A = \\big\\{ f \\in \\mathcal{C}[-1,3] \\: | \\: f(0)=-f(2) \\big\\}.$ \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is closed under function addition\n\n- $A$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\n- $A$ is a subspace of $\\mathcal{C}[-1,3]$\n\n- $A$ is **not** a subspace of $\\mathcal{C}[-1,3]$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If $f(x),g(x) \\in A,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f+g)(0) & =f(0)+g(0) \\\\  & =-f(2)-g(2) \\\\  & =-(f(2)+g(2)) \\\\  & =-(f+g)(2),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means $f+g \\in A.$ So, $A$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is true. If $f(x) \\in A$ and $k \\in \\mathbb{R},$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(kf)(0) & =kf(0) \\\\  & =k\\cdot (-f(2)) \\\\  & =-kf(2) \\\\  & =-(kf)(2),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means $kf \\in A.$ So, $A$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is true. $A$ is a subspace because it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124319 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8f306cf2-6881-5948-90bd-4e0ce98b4116" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subspaces of Matrix Vector Spaces"}] :question/id #uuid "7fed39de-7304-52a0-a94d-e55703175d7d" :question/math-academy-id "e-8233" :question/problem "Consider the vector space $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $\\mathbb{R},$ and its subset $N$ containing all $2\\times 2$ matrices such that the sum of the elements on the main diagonal is greater or equal to $0.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $N$ is closed under matrix addition\n\n- $N$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\n- $N$ is a subspace of $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$\n\n- $N$ is **not** a subspace of $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If $A_1$ and $A_2$ are two matrices from $N,$ then the sum of the elements on the main diagonal for each matrix is greater or equal to $0.$ Therefore, $A_1+A_2$ is a matrix where the sum of the elements on the main diagonal is greater or equal to $0.$ This matrix belongs to $N,$  and so $N$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. If $A$ is an element of $N,$ then the sum of the elements on the main diagonal is greater than or equal to $0.$ If we let $k=-1 \\in \\mathbb{R},$ then $kA$ is a matrix where the sum of the elements on the main diagonal is less than or equal to $0.$ This matrix does not belong to $N,$  and so $N$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $N$ is a *not* subspace because it's not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and IV only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124320 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b1bb974-826a-590f-8d32-cdc868ca827c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subspaces of Polynomial Vector Spaces"}] :question/id #uuid "05151ae0-102c-5ce8-8bc6-082e22aa77d0" :question/math-academy-id "e-8234" :question/problem "Consider the vector space $\\mathbb R_3[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $3,$ and its subset $A = \\big\\{ at^3-bt \\: | \\: a,b \\in\\mathbb{R} \\big\\}.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is closed under polynomial addition\n\n- $A$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\n- $A$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb R_3[t]$\n\n- $A$ is **not** a subspace of $\\mathbb R_3[t]$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If $f(t)=a_1t^3-b_1t$ and $g(t)=a_2t^3-b_2t$ are two polynomials from $A,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)+g(t) & =({a}_{1}{t}^{3}-{b}_{1}t)+({a}_{2}{t}^{3}-{b}_{2}t) \\\\  & =({a}_{1}+{a}_{2}){t}^{3}-({b}_{1}+{b}_{2})t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a polynomial from $A.$ So, $A$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is true. If $f(t)=at^3-bt \\in A$ and $k \\in \\mathbb{R},$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kf(t) & =k(a{t}^{3}-bt) \\\\  & =(ka){t}^{3}-(kb)t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a polynomial from $A.$ So, $A$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is true. $A$ is a subspace because it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350658 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b1bb974-826a-590f-8d32-cdc868ca827c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subspaces of Polynomial Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23748f69-587f-53ef-8dc8-a9f7f3578dd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350660} {:answer/id #uuid "116aba5d-3108-5126-930e-9b95f3df2a62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350661} {:answer/id #uuid "0d7c3e6d-8d14-5705-8193-ea40dc5f72da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350662} {:answer/id #uuid "afc7beb1-5e7f-5b84-99b3-5e64779e7a24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350663} {:answer/id #uuid "5045511f-8b47-5700-b7c4-a4a7515c2050" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350664}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d7c3e6d-8d14-5705-8193-ea40dc5f72da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350662} :answer-field/id #uuid "e596d731-aa76-5197-b9aa-9f951e538408" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350659}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c442e1c1-38ed-511a-a1b1-3b78d327f498" :question/math-academy-id "q-107102" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${R}_{5}[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $5,$ and its subset $A$ containing all polynomials of the form $a{t}^{5}+b,$ where $a,b\\in R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is closed under polynomial addition\n- $A$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $A$ is a subspace of ${R}_{5}[t]$\n- $A$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}_{5}[t]$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If $f(t)={a}_{1}{t}^{5}+{b}_{1}$ and $g(t)={a}_{2}{t}^{5}+{b}_{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)+g(t) & =({a}_{1}{t}^{5}+{b}_{1})+({a}_{2}{t}^{5}+{b}_{2}) \\\\  & =({a}_{1}+{a}_{2}){t}^{5}+({b}_{1}+{b}_{2}),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a polynomial from $A.$ So, $A$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is true. If $f(t)=a{t}^{5}+b$ and $k\\in R,$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kf(t) & =k(a{t}^{5}+b) \\\\  & =(ka){t}^{5}+(kb),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a polynomial from $A.$ So, $A$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is true while statement IV is false. $A$ is a subspace because it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350665 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8f306cf2-6881-5948-90bd-4e0ce98b4116" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subspaces of Matrix Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19e0c5ae-71ff-5ff1-9c66-7ea651f368a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350667} {:answer/id #uuid "e41c6cca-976d-593f-b7d5-fe3cb97c8db1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350668} {:answer/id #uuid "2bb242ec-43cd-5b7d-9620-9f9498b1f51d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350669} {:answer/id #uuid "9689d669-a3a1-5425-9f68-43e17c08bad5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350670} {:answer/id #uuid "c8257309-5396-57ec-8b3c-4ba614e2f0f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350671}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9689d669-a3a1-5425-9f68-43e17c08bad5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350670} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6b30a02-5870-5daa-b785-941270bb098a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350666}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b6393c24-bcf5-559b-abd5-9923066a7bff" :question/math-academy-id "q-107115" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R,$  and its subset $D$ containing all $2\\times 2$ matrices such that the sum of all its entries equals $0.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $D$ is closed under matrix addition\n- $D$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $D$ is a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n- $D$ is **not** a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Suppose ${A}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & {b}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {d}_{1}\\end{aligned}]$ and ${A}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & {b}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2} & {d}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ are two matrices from $D.$  then \n\n$$\n{A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}+{a}_{2} & {b}_{1}+{b}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}+{c}_{2} & {d}_{1}+{d}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n and since \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({a}_{1}+{a}_{2})+({b}_{1}+{b}_{2})+({c}_{1}+{c}_{2})+({d}_{1}+{d}_{2}) & = \\\\ ({a}_{1}+{b}_{1}+{c}_{1}+{d}_{1})+({a}_{1}+{b}_{1}+{c}_{1}+{d}_{1}) & = \\\\ 0+0 & = \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe can conclude that ${A}_{1}+{A}_{2}$ also belongs to $D.$ So, $D$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, suppose $A=[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}]\\in D$ and let $\\alpha \\in R.$ Then \n\n$$\n\\alpha A=[\\begin{aligned}\\alpha a & \\alpha b \\\\ \\alpha c & \\alpha d\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n and so\nthe sum of the entries of $\\alpha A$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha a+\\alpha b+\\alpha c+\\alpha d & =\\alpha (a+b+c+d) \\\\  & =\\alpha \\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $D$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is true, while statement IV is false. $D$ is a subspace because it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350672 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8f306cf2-6881-5948-90bd-4e0ce98b4116" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subspaces of Matrix Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "300a495e-99fd-5f64-9e08-d7621815c6e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350674} {:answer/id #uuid "ae219587-c9dc-5c52-bb40-cbb11d8abf1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350675} {:answer/id #uuid "afcaf0b2-3200-5c14-affc-1c9c5335c664" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350676} {:answer/id #uuid "717f5fc4-237e-54dc-a60f-2761907a2439" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350677} {:answer/id #uuid "58435657-7075-56cd-be99-3bfe19cf0186" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350678}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "717f5fc4-237e-54dc-a60f-2761907a2439" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350677} :answer-field/id #uuid "8674718f-2817-58ee-bde0-ef8febd0d3a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350673}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "148c2629-dc2f-5e60-a1c4-775105bc5816" :question/math-academy-id "q-107119" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R,$  and its subset $T$ containing all $2\\times 2$ matrices of the form $[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ 0 & c\\end{aligned}].$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $T$ is closed under matrix addition\n- $T$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $T$ is a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n- $T$ is **not** a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If ${A}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & {b}_{1} \\\\ 0 & {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}]$ and ${A}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & {b}_{1} \\\\ 0 & {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}]$ are two matrices from $T,$  then \n\n$$\n{A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}+{a}_{2} & {b}_{1}+{b}_{2} \\\\ 0 & {c}_{1}+{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \nis also an element of $T.$ So, $T$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, if $A=[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ 0 & c\\end{aligned}]\\in T$ and $\\alpha \\in R,$ then \n\n$$\n\\alpha A=[\\begin{aligned}\\alpha a & \\alpha b \\\\ 0 & \\alpha c\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nalso belongs to $T.$ So, $T$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is true, while statement IV is false. $T$ is a subspace because it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350679 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef65513d-1e9e-571f-8b93-4e2d63aed68e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subspaces of Function Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65838eda-8de1-5f0d-8ef4-a7b283791f36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350681} {:answer/id #uuid "16674730-14a1-542e-8514-1acfa22a0494" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350682} {:answer/id #uuid "12429499-84d0-550d-9f42-b837166ca3a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350683} {:answer/id #uuid "f3321fe4-a7b3-5efb-81fc-259c75c504b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350684} {:answer/id #uuid "fe75ab53-d27c-5472-bcd4-3b4e2d7dc389" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350685}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "12429499-84d0-550d-9f42-b837166ca3a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350683} :answer-field/id #uuid "011b6369-9b9c-5674-8229-147862164a6c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350680}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8f174cf8-1ff2-5a9d-8c8f-e86d5af28572" :question/math-academy-id "q-107139" :question/problem "Consider the vector space $C[0,1]$ of all continuous functions $f:[0,1]\\to R$ and its subset $N$ containing all functions such that $f(0)=0.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $N$ is closed under function addition\n- $N$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $N$ is a subspace of $C[0,1]$\n- $N$ is **not** a subspace of $C[0,1]$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, suppose $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ are functions from $N.$ Then $f(x)=g(x)=0$ and \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f+g)(0) & =f(0)+g(0) \\\\  & =0+0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $N$ is closed under function addition.\n\n- Statement II is true.  Indeed, suppose $f(x)\\in N$  and let $\\alpha \\in R.$ Then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha f)(0) & =\\alpha \\cdot f(0) \\\\  & =\\alpha \\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $N$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is true, while statement IV is false. $N$ is a subspace because it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350686 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef65513d-1e9e-571f-8b93-4e2d63aed68e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subspaces of Function Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82017a67-416a-5449-9797-0065c3c09b94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350688} {:answer/id #uuid "3115efa1-4c72-5437-9dbe-8f978c696c1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350689} {:answer/id #uuid "976356a3-17b7-5e7e-a6b0-a8972f84be59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350690} {:answer/id #uuid "3e7c5a2a-5137-51e6-9c48-04638a38c680" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350691} {:answer/id #uuid "e00f2b00-a3e1-5b56-80d2-59320a85711e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350692}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e00f2b00-a3e1-5b56-80d2-59320a85711e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350692} :answer-field/id #uuid "0bd6270b-4500-5f08-8991-91368c1227eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350687}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d0b8bb1e-b049-51fa-ae87-6576c2ff6840" :question/math-academy-id "q-107141" :question/problem "Consider the vector space $C[0,1]$ of all continuous functions $f:[0,1]\\to R$ and its subset $W$ containing all polynomials of degree greater than $2.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $W$ is closed under function addition\n- $W$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $W$ is a subspace of $C[0,1]$\n- $W$ is **not** a subspace of $C[0,1]$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if $f(x)={x}^{3}+1$ and $g(x)=-{x}^{3}+1,$ then\n\n$$\nf(x)+g(x)={x}^{3}+1+(-{x}^{3})+1=2,\n$$\n\nwhich is a polynomial of degree zero. So, $W$ is *not* closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, if $f(x)={x}^{3}$ and $k=0,$ then\n\n$$\nkf(x)=0\\cdot {x}^{3}=0.\n$$\n\n \nSo, $W$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $W$ is *not* a subspace because it's not closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125069 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b1bb974-826a-590f-8d32-cdc868ca827c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subspaces of Polynomial Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0c56ca0-5fde-578e-8a11-320867e3c29c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123437} {:answer/id #uuid "e9fdce44-4d82-55cd-b8b7-f25b7d875424" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123438} {:answer/id #uuid "0ad7f7ca-c6b6-5bf7-bd00-cb9d3205e75c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123439} {:answer/id #uuid "71368ba5-9984-5976-8c58-be6f5299b2c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123440} {:answer/id #uuid "7bee109f-1897-5140-ad22-59df9ebc77e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123441}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7bee109f-1897-5140-ad22-59df9ebc77e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123441} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2599b0e-7655-54a7-a465-3b7273682fa8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123436}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f4e607a0-6afc-5179-8279-85636fe2408c" :question/math-academy-id "q-43769" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${R}_{2}[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $2,$ and its subset $B={{t}^{2}+at+b\\,|\\,a,b\\in R}.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $B$ is closed under polynomial addition\n- $B$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $B$ is a subspace of ${R}_{2}[t]$\n- $B$ is a **not** a subspace of ${R}_{2}[t]$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if $f(t)=g(t)={t}^{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)+g(t) & ={t}^{2}+{t}^{2} \\\\  & =2{t}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not a polynomial from $B.$ So, $B$ is *not* closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, if $f(t)={t}^{2}$ and $k=2,$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kf(t) & =2{t}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not a polynomial from $B.$ So, $B$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $B$ is *not* a subspace because it's not closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350693 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b1bb974-826a-590f-8d32-cdc868ca827c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subspaces of Polynomial Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "72922d54-d133-5cec-a5ac-bfdad95891f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350695} {:answer/id #uuid "15001532-d124-5b39-b199-872bbae98bbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350696} {:answer/id #uuid "fc62a19b-f08c-54fd-ab82-43a2c1dd6e4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350697} {:answer/id #uuid "ca051381-d405-57d2-930d-ce60c0f47ae3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350698} {:answer/id #uuid "a32f902f-d232-58f9-b370-6108380bdeaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350699}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca051381-d405-57d2-930d-ce60c0f47ae3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350698} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f303e24-9692-5388-9751-daa52456e183" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350694}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b76e419a-9a41-539f-b5aa-11832f8d9576" :question/math-academy-id "q-43778" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${R}_{3}[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $3,$ and its subset $B={p(t)\\in {R}_{3}[t]\\,|\\,p(1)=1}.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $B$ is closed under polynomial addition\n- $B$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $B$ is a subspace of ${R}_{3}[t]$\n- $B$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}_{3}[t]$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn. \n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if $f(1)=g(1)=1,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1)+g(1) & =1+1 \\\\  & =2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not a polynomial from $B.$ So, $B$ is *not* closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, if $f(1)=1$ and $k=2,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kf(1) & =2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not a polynomial from $B.$ So, $B$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $B$ is *not* a subspace because it's not closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350700 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef65513d-1e9e-571f-8b93-4e2d63aed68e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subspaces of Function Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "568b510f-f182-504f-8472-601d6985ed4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350702} {:answer/id #uuid "26372403-48de-5335-82ba-46032f54180b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350703} {:answer/id #uuid "ce6eb074-dbd4-5af8-9583-ae6b0ee9a86a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350704} {:answer/id #uuid "bf2df6f7-25e1-5c53-b118-286c74536219" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350705} {:answer/id #uuid "688c994a-9a7b-541e-9200-0ac846aa727a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350706}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf2df6f7-25e1-5c53-b118-286c74536219" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350705} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc610545-0d5f-515a-aa2b-b11bd5bf103c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350701}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "767183d2-65f4-5900-a0df-2c9438f756aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-43779" :question/problem "Consider the vector space $C[-1,1]$ of all continuous functions $f:[-1,1]\\to R$ and its subset $B={f\\in C[-1,1]\\,|\\,f(1)=f(-1)}.$ \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $B$ is closed under function addition\n- $B$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $B$ is a subspace of $C[-1,1]$\n- $B$ is **not** a subspace of $C[-1,1]$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If $f(x),g(x)\\in B,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f+g)(1) & =f(1)+g(1) \\\\  & =f(-1)+g(-1) \\\\  & =(f+g)(-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $B$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is true. If $f(x)\\in B$ and $k\\in R,$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(kf)(1) & =kf(1) \\\\  & =kf(-1) \\\\  & =(kf)(-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $B$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is true, while statement IV is false. $B$ is a subspace because it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350707 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b1bb974-826a-590f-8d32-cdc868ca827c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subspaces of Polynomial Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d7ec20e-9590-5257-b6e6-4c1e9dd1bc96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350709} {:answer/id #uuid "dc904574-4999-5913-95db-88609d9da83e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350710} {:answer/id #uuid "537d219c-98b7-539d-b45b-2258da944335" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350711} {:answer/id #uuid "1efbf8be-8cb7-51ee-a0f5-18e3a43ff141" :answe